  "constraintReading": "len(temperatures) can reach 10⁵, so checking every later day for every day, O(n²), could take around 5·10⁹ steps: far too slow. Aim for O(n), where each day is pushed once and popped at most once. In a list that only gets colder, every day waits at the same time, so the stack needs O(n) space.",
  "hints": {
    "clarify": "Does a day with the same temperature count as warmer? No: the next warmer day has to be strictly higher. Try [50, 50, 55] by hand. The first day waits 2 days, because the second day is only as warm; the second day waits 1 day; the last day never sees a warmer one, so its wait is 0. The answer is [2, 1, 0].",
    "approach": "Stack, the monotonic kind. A day stays unanswered until a warmer day comes along, and that warmer day answers every colder day still waiting, newest first. Keep the indexes of the waiting days on a stack; their temperatures never rise from bottom to top, because a warmer day would already have popped the colder ones. For each new day, pop every day on top that is colder, record its wait, then push the new day. Every index is pushed once and popped at most once, so one pass is enough.",
    "whyNot": "Scanning forward from every day until you find a warmer one works, but when the list keeps getting colder, each day scans to the end: O(n²), around 5·10⁹ steps for 10⁵ days. And a stack of temperatures isn't enough: when a day is popped, you need its index to count the wait, so push indexes and look the temperatures up.",
    "slots": {
      "setup": "A list `answer` of zeros, one per day, and an empty list `stack` for the indexes of the days still waiting for a warmer day.",
      "loop": "Visit the days once from left to right with `enumerate`, so you have the index `i` and the temperature `t` together.",
      "update": "While `stack` isn't empty and the day on top of it is colder than `t`, pop that day. After the pops, push `i`: today waits for its own warmer day.",
      "record": "For each index `j` you pop, today is its next warmer day, so its wait is `i` minus `j`. Store that in `answer` at position `j`.",
      "return": "Return `answer`. The days still on `stack` never met a warmer day, so they keep their 0."
    }
  },
  "solution": {
    "code": "class Solution:\n    def dailyTemperatures(self, temperatures: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n        answer = [0] * len(temperatures)\n        stack: list[int] = []\n        for i, t in enumerate(temperatures):\n            while stack and temperatures[stack[-1]] < t:  # viz:compare\n                j = stack.pop()  # viz:resolve\n                answer[j] = i - j\n            stack.append(i)  # viz:push\n        return answer  # viz:done\n",
    "explanation": "The `while` loop can pop several days at once, but every index is pushed exactly once and popped at most once, so all the pops together take O(n) and the whole pass is O(n) time. In a list that only gets colder, nothing is ever popped and all n indexes sit on the stack, so the space is O(n).",
    "toolkit": ["enumerate"]
  },
